      "math_academy_id": "q-133764",
      "knowledge_point_id": "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Trendline",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/f6cbd594db6ab2454844d744ce1b776c3f561c4a6295abb9222ad2416c9f9292.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-8}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+b \\\\ 8 & =-1+b \\\\ 9 & =b \\\\ b & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+9.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
