    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-134018",
      "knowledge_point_id": "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Trendline",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/da0836fa401981d7a0a254a136036a0ead7e3be8eefff5ef3fdfb31070759f0a.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-3}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{4}x+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}(1)+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}+b \\\\ 12 & =5+4b \\\\ 7 & =4b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{3}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+1"
            },
            {
